meta {
  name: Create API Client
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/admin/api-clients
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Admin-Secret: {{admin_secret}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "name": "Example Partner System",
    "description": "Issued for <system name> to call kiwipay's payment APIs"
  }
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  ## Create API Client
  
  Bruno: `admin/Create API Client`
  
  Issues a new `X-Api-Key` / `X-Api-Secret` pair for an external system to call kiwipay's API.
  Every route under `/api/**` requires this pair except the two Intasend webhook callbacks
  (`intasend-transaction/Webhook (Callback)`, `intasend-transaction/Send Money Webhook
  (Callback)`), which stay open since Intasend can't send custom headers.
  
  - **This is the only endpoint here protected by a different mechanism** — `X-Admin-Secret`
    (set the `admin_secret` environment variable to the server's `ADMIN_API_KEY`), not
    `X-Api-Key`/`X-Api-Secret`. That's deliberate: there's no API client yet to authenticate the
    request that creates the first one, so client management needs its own, separate credential.
  - The response's `apiSecret` is shown **once**, in plaintext, only on this create call — it's
    stored hashed (`BCrypt`) server-side and cannot be retrieved again. Copy it immediately into
    the `api_key`/`api_secret` environment variables (used by every other folder's inherited
    headers) or wherever the consuming system stores its credentials. If it's lost, revoke this
    client and create a new one.
  - `apiKey` is safe to log/display (e.g. `kp_xxxxx`); `apiSecret` is not.
  
  Code source: `ApiClientControllerImpl.createApiClient` / `ApiClientServiceImpl.createApiClient`.
}
